## Chapter 1

That day, for the first time, I looked at everything with full eyes. The trees, the chatter of birds, the touch of the wind, and the scent of nature that could hold the mind spellbound. It felt as though I were seeing it all for the very first time.

My mind would not rest at home. A fierce wish rose to step outside and feel all of this with my skin, with my breath. For the first time I felt the sun on my palms, the wind against my face. Everything was so full of joy that inside me there was only gratitude—a boundless sense of wonder and thankfulness toward every thing: trees, vines, wind, sun, every part of the living world.

It felt as if I had come home; as if I too were weaving a nest with these birds; as if I too were flowing like the wind, unafraid.

I wandered the lanes of the village all day like that, looking at everything—as if after many lifetimes I were seeing it again today. To look was so sweet, like watching a small child at play: unselfish, wholly present.

I did not know what was happening to me, but whatever it was, I did not want to change it, nor did I wish to pin it down in words. The questions within slowly faded; I no longer even wanted answers. I only wanted—to scatter fragrance with these flowers, to dance and sing as if all of nature were singing and dancing, and I had stepped into the same rhythm.
